            <div xml:id="d13">

                <pb facs="0142" n="10" sameAs="frus:frus1926v01/pg10"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.C114/488: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Tuck</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-04-06T17:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 6, 1926—5
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received April 6—3:38
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Substance Department’s telegram of April 3, 5 p.m., informally brought to the
                    attention of member of judicial section [of] Secretariat, who stated, as his
                    personal opinion, that while there exist no express provisions in the protocol
                    and in the absence of precedent, he considered it very doubtful whether member
                    state of League, signatory of Court protocol, would automatically be deprived of
                    its status of signatory of protocol by withdrawal from League.</p>
                <p>Assuming this to be the case, member state, in order to withdraw from Permanent
                    Court, might resort to one of two methods: (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>)
                    denunciation <pb facs="0143" n="11" xml:id="pg11"/> of protocol; (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">b</hi>) invite other signatories to release it from its
                    obligations in this connection these conditions [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >sic</hi>].</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Tuck</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
